ther (technical), excellent computer skills (technical). Concerns: grumpy, uncooperative (crew cooperation). No info on decision quality, attention, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: strengths: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress), concentration (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: arrogant (crew cooperation/communication), weak leadership (crew cooperation). No info on reliability, decision quality, adaptability.\n\nWhich candidate best fits overall? The panel must consider all criteria. Candidate A has strong reliability and decision quality, which are crucial. Communication difficulty could be mitigated. Egocentric attitude may be a risk. Candidate C has strong situational awareness and leadership, but unorganized and poor feedback orientation could be safety-critical. Which is more severe? In a safety-critical role, reliability and consistent behavior are paramount. Candidate A is 100% reliable. Candidate C's reliability not mentioned. Also attention: we have no explicit attention for C, but we have unorganized. Candidate A has \"excellent attention skills\" from Agent 3 notes, but not in our own notes. However we can reference that as known from other panel members? We can use that as evidence if we have it? The instruction says we can use facts from other panel members if they have been shared publicly. Agent 3 said \"Candidate A has excellent attention skills\". That is now public. So we can use that.\n\nThus Candidate A has reliability, attention, decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence, positive crew atmosphere. Concerns: communication difficulty, egocentric, limited adaptability. Candidate C lacks reliability evidence, has attention concerns, feedback issues.\n\nThus I might shift to Candidate A. Provide argument referencing Agent 2's points and Agent 3's earlier shift. I can say I now support Candidate A because reliability and decision quality are top priority, and communication can be trained.\n\nThus my vote: Candidate A.\n\nNow produce public message: reference Agent 2's argument, note that while C's anticipation is strong, lack of proven reliability and attention is a gap. Emphasize A's 100% reliability, quick correct decisions, calm in crisis, positive crew atmosphere, excellent attention (citing Agent 3). A's concerns: communication difficulty and egocentric, but can be mitigated with training. Conclude vote for A.\n\nNow produce METADATA_JSON with vote Candidate A.\n\nProceed."}
